{"ts":"2026-09-26T15:35:05.957Z","session":"c47cae16-279d-41a2-b401-3efa2780e299","event":"llm.start","callId":"cd481a65-561d-490a-8412-5e448db248f7","name":"gemini.generate","args":["Answer the research question using ONLY the evidence below.\nRules:\n- Every claim must be directly stated by a source. \"citedUrl\" is the URL of the best source for it; \"supportingUrls\" lists EVERY source URL that states it (including citedUrl).\n- If the evidence does not answer something the question asks, put it in \"unableToFind\". Never fill gaps from your own knowledge and never guess.\n- \"entitiesLearned\": named entities with facts established by the evidence, as {\"name\", \"type\", \"data\": {key facts}}.\n- \"answer\" summarises only what the claims support.\n\nReturn JSON exactly in this shape:\n{\"answer\": string, \"claims\": [{\"text\": string, \"citedUrl\": string, \"supportingUrls\": string[]}], \"entitiesLearned\": [{\"name\": string, \"type\": string, \"data\": object}], \"unableToFind\": string[]}\n\nQuestion: Which company recently raised funding in Indian quick commerce?\nSub-questions to cover:\n- Which Indian quick commerce companies have raised funding recently?\n- What are the details of the latest funding rounds in the Indian quick commerce sector?\n\nEvidence:\n[S1] Indian quick commerce raises Rs 335.32 Crore in 2025, market value to reach Rs 29,300 crore | Debonkar Roy posted on the topic | LinkedIn\nURL: https://www.linkedin.com/posts/debonkar-roymarketing_debcreates-india-strategy-activity-7363066691586592769-L8yk\n87 Indian companies have raised Rs 335.32 Crore in 2025 so far for quick commerce! In 2024, quick commerce secured Rs 11,620 crore in funding. The sector is evolving beyond food and grocery delivery, expanding into electronics, household services, and ultra-fast fulfillment promises ranging from 10 minutes to 1 hour. Here are 5 facts about the Indian quick commerce market: 1. Current market value is expected to reach Rs 29,300 crore in 2025. 2. Projected growth for quick commerce space is >4.5% [...] unit economics by leading players. -Expansion beyond metro cities. - Diversification into new product categories. Despite the funding crunch globally, India's venture capital market rebounded in 2024, hitting Rs 1,15,000 crore in funding. Blinkit, Instamart, Zepto, bigbasket, and Dunzo Daily are the major companies operating in this market. With 50+ quick commerce startups, India leads globally, followed by the US with 15 startups, and the UK with 13. The presence of 87 companies in the funding [...] space is >4.5% CAGR to reach Rs 36,500 crore by 2030. 3. It is projected to grow by 16.07% (2025-2029) resulting in a market volume of Rs 8,20,000 crore in 2029. 4. According to Redseer, industry experts predict a 40-45% GMV CAGR for the next 3 years. 5. According to Bain, India's quick commerce market will surge over 40% annually until 2030. The quick commerce sector witnessed a funding boom since 2024 due to: - Investor confidence in the ultra-fast delivery model. - Proven unit eco\n\n[S2] Quick Commerce India: Funding Hits $40.4M in 2025 After $1.4B Record in 2024\nURL: https://www.deccanherald.com/business/with-87-companies-in-indias-quick-commerce-segment-raises-404-million-in-2025-3684626\nFeatures\n\nHealth\n\nEducation\n\nTechnology\n\nDH Brandspot\n\nSubscribe\n\nMenu\n\nGo Ad Lite\n\nAdvertisement.\n\nStory continues below. Go Ad Lite\n\nHomebusiness\n\n# With 87 companies in India's quick commerce, segment raises $40.4 million in 2025\n\nAccording to market intelligence firm Tracxn, there are 87 companies in the quick commerce space in India and in 2025 alone.\n\nUma Kannan\n\nLast Updated : 17 August 2025, 11:32IST\n\nStory continues below. Go Ad Lite\n\nJoin Us\n\nPrefer\n\nComments\n\nAdvertisement. Go Ad Lite [...] Published 17 August 2025, 11:32IST\n\nBusiness NewsSwiggyBlinkitOnline shopping\n\n## Follow us on :\n\n## Follow Us\n\n[S3] Quick Commerce - 2026 Market & Investments Trends - Tracxn\nURL: https://tracxn.com/d/trending-business-models/startups-in-quick-commerce/__8Em61CsQQeo5EmPsP-wGXgDzh5yotwlRuXpxOVdqw6Y\n| Year | Total Funding |\n --- |\n| 2026 | $507M |\n| 2025 | $2.06B |\n| 2024 | $2B |\n| 2023 | $1.08B |\n| 2022 | $3.28B |\n| 2021 | $11.9B |\n| 2020 | $3.64B |\n| 2019 | $3.84B |\n| 2018 | $4.79B |\n| 2017 | $1.79B |\n\n### Total funding across countries in Quick Commerce\n\nIn the last 10 years, Quick Commerce startups in United States have recieved the highest funding among all countries - $10B. India follows with $8.9B and Germany with $2.8B. [...] | Country | Total Funding |\n --- |\n| United States | $10B |\n| India | $8.9B |\n| Germany | $2.8B |\n| United Kingdom | $1.76B |\n| Saudi Arabia | $286M |\n| Indonesia | $148M |\n| Australia | $23M |\n| Philippines | $21.1M |\n| Canada | $9.1M |\n| Italy | $3M |\n\n### Total funding amount raised by stage in Quick Commerce\n\nOver the last 10 years, Quick Commerce have seen $242M into Seed Stage funding rounds, $4.24B into Early Stage funding rounds and $26.2B go into Late Stage funding rounds. [...] | Company Stage | No. of companies |\n --- |\n| Founded | 256 |\n| Funded | 105 |\n| Series A+ | 55 |\n| Series B+ | 41 |\n| Series C+ | 35 |\n\n## Funding Trends in Quick Commerce\n\n### Total funding year-on-year in Quick Commerce\n\nQuick Commerce saw a total funding of more than $34.9B in the last 10 years. The most amount of funding was in 2021 at more than $11.9B. There has been a total funding of $507M in 2026 till date.\n\n[S4] Indian quick commerce startup Zepto files for $1.2 billion IPO amid warnings of bubble in the sector\nURL: https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/27/indias-zepto-files-for-ipo-as-quick-commerce-race-heats-up.html\nWalmart-owned Flipkart also launched its quick commerce service in 2024. Indian food delivery companies, Swiggy and Eternal, which owns Zomato and Blinkit, were among the first movers in this space.\n\nOver the last three to five years, several companies have entered the quick commerce market in India and are \" indulging in a price war\" as firms chase a large total addressable market, Taurani said. [...] Indian quick-commerce startup Zepto has confidentially filed for an initial public offering, according to a public notice issued by the company on Sunday.\n\nThe company plans to raise 110 billion rupees ($1.22 billion) of fresh capital. Zepto was valued at $7 billion during its last funding round in October, data from research firm Tracxn showed.\n\nZepto told CNBC it filed its draft prospectus under the confidential route, which keeps its filings private, and declined to disclose further details. [...] Quick commerce, which promises deliveries in as little as 10 minutes, has become one of India's most competitive consumer internet sectors.\n\nWhile e-commerce giant Amazon has long offered same-day delivery services in India through \"Amazon Fresh\", the launch of its 15-minute \"Amazon Now\" service in June marked an aggressive shift into a crowded space.\n\nThe Jeff Bezos-owned U.S. company has started its quick commerce operations across Indian's three major cities—Mumbai, Delhi and Bengaluru.\n\n[S5] Indian quick commerce startup Zepto files for $1.2 billion IPO amid warnings of bubble in the sector\nURL: https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/27/indias-zepto-files-for-ipo-as-quick-commerce-race-heats-up.html?msockid=0c2f936e68336de43ee685be69e56c05\nWalmart-owned Flipkart also launched its quick commerce service in 2024. Indian food delivery companies, Swiggy and Eternal, which owns Zomato and Blinkit, were among the first movers in this space.\n\nOver the last three to five years, several companies have entered the quick commerce market in India and are \" indulging in a price war\" as firms chase a large total addressable market, Taurani said. [...] Indian quick-commerce startup Zepto has confidentially filed for an initial public offering, according to a public notice issued by the company on Sunday.\n\nThe company plans to raise 110 billion rupees ($1.22 billion) of fresh capital. Zepto was valued at $7 billion during its last funding round in October, data from research firm Tracxn showed.\n\nZepto told CNBC it filed its draft prospectus under the confidential route, which keeps its filings private, and declined to disclose further details. [...] Quick commerce, which promises deliveries in as little as 10 minutes, has become one of India's most competitive consumer internet sectors.\n\nWhile e-commerce giant Amazon has long offered same-day delivery services in India through \"Amazon Fresh\", the launch of its 15-minute \"Amazon Now\" service in June marked an aggressive shift into a crowded space.\n\nThe Jeff Bezos-owned U.S. company has started its quick commerce operations across Indian's three major cities—Mumbai, Delhi and Bengaluru.\n\n[S6] The second wave of quick commerce is here | Anuj Suvarna\nURL: https://www.linkedin.com/posts/anuj-suvarna_the-second-wave-of-quick-commerce-is-here-activity-7369230316722405378-J7cd\nThe second wave of quick commerce is here FirstClub has raised $23 million in Series A funding at a valuation of about $120 million, less than three months after launch. The round was led by Accel and RTP Global, with participation from Blume Founders Fund, 2am VC, Paramark Ventures, and Aditya Birla Ventures. Founded by Ayyappan R (ex-Flipkart, Myntra, and former CEO of Cleartrip), FirstClub calls itself India’s first “quality-first” quick commerce platform — a sharp contrast to Blinkit, [...] Partners. Fashion: Slikk, Knot, and Zulu Club raised capital to deliver clothing and accessories within an hour. Food: WAAYU ($2M) is building a zero-commission delivery platform; Zing is betting on ultra-fast fresh meals. Healthcare: Farmako raised funds to deliver medicines in under 30 minutes. Infrastructure: Kuik is building shared dark stores for brands, with a $3–4M round in the works. The message is clear: Quick commerce in India is evolving from “anything in 10 minutes” to vertical-led [...] less than three months since its inception.AccelandRTP Globalled the round, with participation from Blume Founders Fund, 2am VC,Paramark Ventures, andAditya Birla Ventures. Read More: \n\nImage 11: FirstClub Raises $23 Million To Fuel Quality-Led Quick Commerce Expansion FirstClub Raises $23 Million To Fuel Quality-Led Quick Commerce Expansion startup.siliconindia.com\n\nImage 12Image 13 291 Comment\n\nLikeComment\n\n Share \n       Copy\n       LinkedIn\n       Facebook\n       X\n\n[S7] Quick-commerce FirstClub doubles valuation to $255M in 9 months | TechCrunch\nURL: https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/03/firstclub-doubles-valuation-to-255m-in-nine-months-on-quality-first-grocery-bet\nThe Bengaluru-based startup has raised $55 million in a Series B round co-led by Peak XV Partners and Sofina, valuing the company at $255 million after the investment. That’s up from $120 million when it last raised capital in September 2025. Existing investors Accel, RTP Global, and Paramark Ventures also participated. The latest financing brings FirstClub’s total funding to $86 million. [...] In a quick-commerce market obsessed with speed, Indian startup FirstClub has convinced investors that quality may be a fresh opportunity, helping to double its valuation just nine months after its last funding round. [...] As grocery shopping increasingly moves online, India’s quick-commerce market has expanded rapidly, growing from about $6.2 billion in FY25 to an estimated $11 billion to $12 billion in FY26, according to a recent ICICI Securities report. Leading players have popularized online grocery shopping through ever-faster deliveries. However, FirstClub is wagering that a growing segment of consumers will prioritize quality and product curation over receiving orders as quickly as possible.\n\n[S8] Quick Commerce CAN'T SURVIVE Like This - Indian Startups News 291\nURL: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vC0p8AHIvKQ\nIn our Quick Updates, we cover:\nUnacademy admitting an 85% valuation drop and exploring M&A,\nVirat Kohli selling One8 to Agilitas Sports while joining as co-founder,\nUber launching Uber Direct with ONDC to enter B2B logistics,\nand strong IPO debuts from Meesho and Aequs.\n\nFinally, in our Weekly Funding News, we break down the $86 million raised this week — including rounds for Fibe, Smart Joules, Elecbits, Dr. Paws, and NeoSapien. [...] [9:34] brain for you. This device basically listens to you the entire day and that helps you recall, organize, and make better decisions. And they have raised\n[9:42] $2 million in their seed round. All right, that's all the startup news I have for you this week. Thank you so much for watching and I'll see you in the next [...] Then, we look at a major development in India’s innovation ecosystem: IIT Bombay launching a ₹250 crore venture fund dedicated entirely to deep-tech. From semiconductors and robotics to biotech, AI, and new materials, this fund could become a crucial link between research and commercialization—taking India one step closer to building globally competitive deep-tech companies.\n\n[S9] Amazon pilots quick-commerce service in India\nURL: https://finance.yahoo.com/news/amazon-pilots-quick-commerce-india-091232017.html\nTitle: Amazon pilots quick-commerce service in India\n2. Best 0% APR cards. 3. Best balance transfer cards. 2. Best high-yield savings accounts. 3. Best money market accounts. # Amazon pilots quick-commerce service in India. Amazon pilots quick-commerce service in India · TechCrunch. Amazon on Tuesday said it is piloting a quick commerce service in India that will see the U.S. tech giant delivering grocery and other items in 15 minutes or less. The firm is the sixth major entrant in India's quick-commerce market, which is currently clocking more than $6 billion in annual sales. Amazon said it is initially piloting the service in Bengaluru. \"Our strategy has always focused on 'Selection, Value and Convenience' and our vision is to build a large profitable business in India,\" said Samir Kumar, the new country manager of Amazon India, in a statement. \"So, while we focus on implementing our strategy to offer the largest selection at fastest speeds and greatest value to customers in every single pin-code across the country, we are excited to start a pilot to give our customers a choice to get their everyday essentials in 15 min or less.\". The quick-commerce model — delivering items to customers within 10 to 15 minutes — hasn't worked in most parts of the world, but it's increasingly finding success in India, where a range of retailers and internet firms, from food delivery giant Swiggy to online cosmetics platform Nykaa, are gearing up their supply chain ecosystems to accommodate f\n\n[S10] Deccan Herald - According to #market intelligence firm...\nURL: https://www.facebook.com/deccanherald/posts/according-to-market-intelligence-firm-tracxn-there-are-87-companies-in-the-quick/1241701744664887\nQuick Commerce India: Funding Hits $40.4M in 2025 After $1.4B Record in 2024\n\n[S11] Indian quick commerce startup Zepto files for $1.2 billion IPO amid warnings of bubble in the sector\nURL: https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/29/zepto-confidential-ipo-filing-india-quick-commerce-instamart-swiggy-blinkit-zomato-eternal-flipkart-amazon.html?msockid=204eacd0836e69a202fdba03820968af\nWalmart-owned Flipkart also launched its quick commerce service in 2024. Indian food delivery companies, Swiggy and Eternal, which owns Zomato and Blinkit, were among the first movers in this space.\n\nOver the last three to five years, several companies have entered the quick commerce market in India and are \" indulging in a price war\" as firms chase a large total addressable market, Taurani said. [...] Indian quick-commerce startup Zepto has confidentially filed for an initial public offering, according to a public notice issued by the company on Sunday.\n\nThe company plans to raise 110 billion rupees ($1.22 billion) of fresh capi