{"ts":"2026-09-26T16:39:38.400Z","session":"3ff3d1fc-beb5-47dd-b9f2-56ad3cf3b200","event":"llm.start","callId":"020e8309-ca34-4a6e-8da9-bf688d52ba38","name":"gemini.generate","args":["Answer the research question using ONLY the evidence below.\nRules:\n- Every claim must be directly stated by a source. \"citedUrl\" is the URL of the best source for it; \"supportingUrls\" lists EVERY source URL that states it (including citedUrl).\n- If the evidence does not answer something the question asks, put it in \"unableToFind\". Never fill gaps from your own knowledge and never guess.\n- \"entitiesLearned\": named entities with facts established by the evidence. Each fact is one short, self-contained statement (e.g. \"CEO and MD of Infosys since 2 January 2018\") with \"sourceUrl\" set to the URL of the one source that states it. Sources headed \"Previously learned\" are facts from earlier research — cite their URL like any other source.\n- \"answer\" summarises only what the claims support.\n\nReturn JSON exactly in this shape:\n{\"answer\": string, \"claims\": [{\"text\": string, \"citedUrl\": string, \"supportingUrls\": string[]}], \"entitiesLearned\": [{\"name\": string, \"type\": string, \"facts\": [{\"fact\": string, \"sourceUrl\": string}]}], \"unableToFind\": string[]}\n\nQuestion: Has [real investor name from Q4] backed any other Indian quick-commerce company besides the one already found?\nSub-questions to cover:\n- Who is the real investor identified in Q4?\n- Which Indian quick-commerce companies has this investor backed?\n- What are the major Indian quick-commerce companies currently operating?\n\nEvidence:\n[S1] Top 5 Quick Commerce Brands in India (2026): Market Leaders | Hyperlink InfoSystem\nURL: https://www.hyperlinkinfosystem.com/article/who-is-winning-quick-commerce-race\nIn 2024, Zepto will be one of India's fastest-growing quick commerce companies. They took the vantage of a mobile app development company and pioneered the notion of 10-minute grocery delivery. Founded by two young entrepreneurs, Aadit Palicha and Kaivalya Vohra, Zepto has rapidly established a niche by prioritizing ultra-fast grocery and basic deliveries, leveraging hyper-local dark shops. Most of these dark stores are strategically positioned in high-demand locations of metropolitan cities [...] In deliveries of 2024, the Indian market is dominated by 2 categories: food delivery via portals like Swiggy and Zomato, and quick commerce with apps like Blinkit, BBDaily, and many more. Even if you consult with a top website development company, the first thing they talk about in quick commerce app development is their highly advanced tech stack, incredible logistics, and a huge network of fulfillment centers. Major companies also use AI, automation, and big data analytics to detect purchase [...] Zepto: Known for its ingenious practice and instantaneous scaling.\n\nBlinkit: #1 quick commerce app in India 2024, and a strong competitor with vast city coverage.\n\nSwiggy Instamart: Utilizes Swiggy’s comprehensive logistics grid.\n\nDunzo: Efficacious in certain metro markets and delivering versatile assistance.\n\nBB Now: A relatively new product from the legendary BigBasket, but a promising contender backed by its established label and also TATA.\n\n[S2] Indian quick commerce startup Zepto files for $1.2 billion IPO amid warnings of bubble in the sector\nURL: https://www.cnbc.com/2025/12/27/indias-zepto-files-for-ipo-as-quick-commerce-race-heats-up.html\nWalmart-owned Flipkart also launched its quick commerce service in 2024. Indian food delivery companies, Swiggy and Eternal, which owns Zomato and Blinkit, were among the first movers in this space.\n\nOver the last three to five years, several companies have entered the quick commerce market in India and are \" indulging in a price war\" as firms chase a large total addressable market, Taurani said.\n\n[S3] India Quick Commerce Market Report, Market Size, Market Major Players, 10-minute grocery platforms - Nexdigm\nURL: https://www.nexdigm.com/market-research/report-store/india-quick-commerce-market-report\nThe India Quick Commerce market is segmented into Grocery, Snacks & Beverages, Fresh Produce, Personal Care & Beauty, and Household Essentials. Snacks & Beverages dominate with ~32% market share in 2024 due to high frequency of impulse buying, portability, and low perishability. Young professionals and students increasingly use quick commerce for convenience, with players like Blinkit and Zepto heavily promoting this category through bundles and offers.\n\n### By Fulfilment Model [...] ## Market Overview\n\nThe India quick commerce market was valued at USD 3.05 billion in FY 2024, up from approximately USD 1.6 billion in FY 2023. This rapid expansion is driven by rising smartphone penetration (77%), faster mobile internet, increasing per capita digital spending, and consumers’ eagerness for instant delivery of daily essentials. [...] Dominated by Dark Stores, accounting for ~48% share in 2024. Quick commerce players prioritize controlling inventory, ensuring 10‑minute delivery through dense, strategically located micro-warehouses. This model enables improved stock accuracy, faster fulfilment, and better margins versus asset-light hyperlocal partnerships.\n\n## Competitive Landscape\n\n[S4] Top 7 Quick Commerce Companies In India: Meaning, Apps 2026\nURL: https://www.ithinklogistics.com/blog/quick-commerce-companies-in-india\nA: Yes. Flipkart launched Flipkart Minutes in 2024, its direct entry into quick commerce. What sets it apart from Blinkit or Zepto is its product mix.\n\nFlipkart Minutes focuses on electronics, phone accessories, chargers, home essentials, and groceries. Backed by Walmart’s supply chain, it delivers in 10–20 minutes through local dark stores.\n\n### Q.6: Is Meesho Quick Commerce? [...] Founded: 2024 | HQ: Bangalore, India, Market Share: <3% (Emerging)\n\nFlipkart’s answer to the q-commerce wave and the most interesting non-grocery play in the space. Flipkart Minutes uses dark stores to deliver electronics, phone accessories, chargers, and home essentials in 10–20 minutes.\n\nBacked by Walmart’s supply chain muscle and Flipkart’s existing seller base. If your SKUs go beyond groceries, this is the platform to watch.\n\n### JioMart Express\n\nFounded: 2022 | HQ: Navi Mumbai, India, [...] | Swiggy Instamart | Swiggy | 2020 | Bangalore | 15–25 min | Swiggy One cross-platform ecosystem | Brands wanting food + grocery bundled visibility |\n| BigBasket BB Now | Tata Group | 2011 | Bangalore | 15–30 min | Grocery depth, Tata supply chain | Grocery-first brands, staples, bulk essentials |\n| Flipkart Minutes | Walmart / Flipkart | 2024 | Bangalore | 10–20 min | Non-grocery play — electronics, home | Sellers with electronics, accessories, home SKUs |\n\n[S5] India's top quick commerce platforms processed 4.45 million ...\nURL: https://www.linkedin.com/posts/livemint_quickcommerce-india-ecommerce-activity-7325147196805337088-YaDF\nIt nearly doubled its volume, growing 90% from 0.85 to 0.95 million orders a day in March 2024 to 1.65 to 1.75 million orders a day in March to reunify. Zepto, on the other hand, saw the fastest growth, tripling its daily order count by 200% from 0.45 to 0.55 million to 1.45 to 1.55 million orders a day, fueled by an aggressive expansion strategy. So against Tamad grew 60% from 0.65 to 0.75 million to 1.05 to 1.15 million orders a day. It's relatively modest growth is attributed to reduce [...] India's top quick commerce platforms Blanket, Zepto and Swiggy in Samarth together fulfilled between 4.15 and 4.45 million orders daily across the country in March 2025, according to data shared with Money Control by industry insiders. Blanket, which is owned by Zomato, maintained its position as the market leader with approximately 1.65 to 1. 75 million orders daily ahead of Zapdos 1.4. 5 to 1.55 million, and Swaggy estimates 1.05 to 1.15 million. Compared to March 2024, when the top three [...] to reduce spending ahead of Swaggie. Maybe you? Zappos user acquisition slowed significantly from 7.88 million in December 2024 to 2.16 million in March, while Swiggy Instrumart acquired 3.3 million new users in March 2025 in comparison to 1.24 million in December 2024, whereas Blanket experienced A slight tip from 5.11 million to 4.9 million new users.\n\n[S6] Zepto raises $450M at $7B valuation as Indian quick-commerce market heats up\nURL: https://finance.yahoo.com/news/zepto-raises-450-million-7-101211041.html\nThe investment is a notable one for CalPERS. The pension fund typically invests in venture capital through intermediary funds rather than leading direct investments in startups. CalPERS has been aggressively expanding its venture exposure since 2022 after what officials called a \"lost decade\" of underperformance, scaling its venture allocation from around $800 million to a targeted $5 billion. The fund's decision to lead a round in an Indian quick-commerce startup seemingly signals strong [...] -0.29%\n\nIndian quick-commerce startup Zepto said it has raised $400 million in a funding round led by a new investor, California Public Employees' Retirement System (CalPERS), a U.S.-based pension fund. The round, which is a mix of primary and secondary investment, also has participation from existing investors, including Avenir, Avra, Lightspeed, Glade Brook, The Stepstone Group, and Nexus Venture Partners. After this funding, the company plans to go public next year. [...] signals strong institutional confidence in India's rapid delivery sector and perhaps CalPERS' growing appetite for direct venture investments in emerging markets, too. Notably, CalPERS is also an investor in funds of Zepto's existing backers, like Lightspeed and General Catalyst.\n\n[S7] Revolutionizing Quick Commerce: Beyond Groceries in India with Massive VC Funding, ETRetail\nURL: https://retail.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/e-commerce/e-tailing/quick-commerce-expands-beyond-groceries-with-vc-funding-but-scale-questions-persist/129915540\nWho survives?  \n  \nAs these verticals expanded, about $500 million was raised across 26 rounds in 2025, with venture capital firms such as Lightspeed India, Stellaris Venture, Nexus Venture Partners, Bain Capital Ventures and Accel backing the continued momentum in quick commerce. [...] in the quick commerce segment raised $586 million across verticals between January 2025 and March 2026, according to Tracxn data. Companies that have raised funds or are in discussions with investors include FirstClub, Swish, Knot, Dazzl, Plazza, Ozi and HomeRun. [...] \"What separates the survivors from the ones that shut down is whether speed is the entire product or just the entry point. Zing and several of the fashion players that didn't make it were essentially fast versions of existing platforms,\" said Sunitha Viswanathan, partner, Kae Capital.  \n  \nKae Capital has invested in Knot, a rapid fashion delivery startup, which operates in a segment that has seen the mergence of multiple startups, including Slikk, Zulu Club and Zilo.\n\n[S8] The second wave of quick commerce is here | Anuj Suvarna\nURL: https://www.linkedin.com/posts/anuj-suvarna_the-second-wave-of-quick-commerce-is-here-activity-7369230316722405378-J7cd\nThe second wave of quick commerce is here FirstClub has raised $23 million in Series A funding at a valuation of about $120 million, less than three months after launch. The round was led by Accel and RTP Global, with participation from Blume Founders Fund, 2am VC, Paramark Ventures, and Aditya Birla Ventures. Founded by Ayyappan R (ex-Flipkart, Myntra, and former CEO of Cleartrip), FirstClub calls itself India’s first “quality-first” quick commerce platform — a sharp contrast to Blinkit, [...] to Blinkit, Zepto, and Swiggy Instamart. “Quick commerce today is built for speed, not standards. At FirstClub, we’re changing that,” Ayyappan said. “Every product you see on our platform is tested, vetted, and curated. Consumers shouldn’t have to compromise between convenience and quality.” But this isn’t just about one startup. India’s quick commerce market is fragmenting into specialized verticals beyond groceries: Babycare: Peeko raised $3.2M led by Stellaris Venture Partners. Fashion: [...] Investors: Stellaris Venture Partners, Maninder Gulati (Lightspeed), Abhishek Goyal, Nitin Gupta, Arjun Vaidya, Kunal Bahl, Rohit Bansal 🔎 What is Peeko solving? Quick commerce in India has been a double-edged sword – hypergrowth but unsustainable burn. Consumers love instant delivery, but players struggle with margins. Peeko is reimagining Q-commerce beyond just “10-minute groceries.” It’s building a platform that prioritizes: Smart dark store networks 🏬 for optimized fulfillment. AI-driven\n\n[S9] Amazon to invest $3Bn into quick commerce in India as it plays catch up - The Tech Portal\nURL: https://thetechportal.com/2026/09/24/amazon-to-invest-3bn-into-quick-commerce-in-india-as-it-plays-catch-up\nIndia’s quick commerce sector has moved from a venture capital experiment to a genuine structural shift in urban retail faster than almost anyone anticipated. Monthly GMV crossed ₹11,000 crore in January 2026 alone — roughly doubling year on year — on approximately 7.8 million orders a day, according to Redseer data. The sector is currently valued at around $19 billion and is projected by Datum Intelligence to reach $41 billion by 2030, growing at 40-45% annually. By that point, quick commerce [...] Amazon plans to invest up to $3 billion in India’s quick commerce market by 2030, Reuters reported today, citing people familiar with the matter. The capital will be deployed in two phases — approximately $1 billion through 2027, followed by a further $2 billion to build out infrastructure and logistics through the end of the decade. [...] The investment, though not publicly announced, shows one of the first firm admissions from Amazon, on how far left behind it is, in a segment that many thought wouldn’t last beyond VC investments. To start with, Amazon will bank on daily essentials, AI-driven demand forecasting, and a rapid expansion of the Amazon Now dark store network from roughly 750 locations today to approximately 1,300 by April next year.\n\n[S10] Quick Commerce - 2026 Market & Investments Trends\nURL: https://tracxn.com/d/trending-business-models/startups-in-quick-commerce/__8Em61CsQQeo5EmPsP-wGXgDzh5yotwlRuXpxOVdqw6Y\n| Country | Total Funding |\n --- |\n| United States | $10B |\n| India | $8.9B |\n| Germany | $2.8B |\n| United Kingdom | $1.76B |\n| Saudi Arabia | $286M |\n| Indonesia | $148M |\n| Australia | $23M |\n| Philippines | $21.1M |\n| Canada | $9.1M |\n| Italy | $3M |\n\n### Total funding amount raised by stage in Quick Commerce\n\nOver the last 10 years, Quick Commerce have seen $242M into Seed Stage funding rounds, $4.24B into Early Stage funding rounds and $26.2B go into Late Stage funding rounds. [...] | Year | Total Funding |\n --- |\n| 2026 | $507M |\n| 2025 | $2.06B |\n| 2024 | $2B |\n| 2023 | $1.08B |\n| 2022 | $3.28B |\n| 2021 | $11.9B |\n| 2020 | $3.64B |\n| 2019 | $3.84B |\n| 2018 | $4.79B |\n| 2017 | $1.79B |\n\n### Total funding across countries in Quick Commerce\n\nIn the last 10 years, Quick Commerce startups in United States have recieved the highest funding among all countries - $10B. India follows with $8.9B and Germany with $2.8B. [...] | Company Stage | No. of companies |\n --- |\n| Founded | 256 |\n| Funded | 105 |\n| Series A+ | 55 |\n| Series B+ | 41 |\n| Series C+ | 35 |\n\n## Funding Trends in Quick Commerce\n\n### Total funding year-on-year in Quick Commerce\n\nQuick Commerce saw a total funding of more than $34.9B in the last 10 years. The most amount of funding was in 2021 at more than $11.9B. There has been a total funding of $507M in 2026 till date.",{"json":true,"schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"answer":{"type":"string"},"claims":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"text":{"type":"string"},"citedUrl":{"type":"string"},"supportingUrls":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}}},"required":["text","citedUrl","supportingUrls"]}},"entitiesLearned":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"name":{"type":"string"},"type":{"type":"string"},"facts":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"fact":{"type":"string"},"sourceUrl":{"type":"string"}},"required":["fact","sourceUrl"]}}},"required":["name","type","facts"]}},"unableToFind":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}}},"required":["answer","claims","entitiesLearned","unableToFind"]}}]}
