{"ts":"2026-09-26T17:17:21.973Z","session":"fb606d91-84d8-4449-ae8e-447eaa3b9089","event":"tool.end","callId":"aecca7f2-6281-4c2e-b1b8-ac09dfedfc57","name":"webSearch","duration_ms":2736,"result":[{"title":"Flipkart Minutes quadruples business as quick-commerce battle intensifies","url":"https://www.business-standard.com/companies/news/flipkart-minutes-quadruples-business-as-quick-commerce-battle-intensifies-126091001430_1.html","content":"Flipkart Minutes, the quick-commerce arm of e-commerce giant Flipkart, has quadrupled its business over the past year as it marks two years since its launch in August 2024. The service now operates nearly 1,200 micro-fulfilment centres across more than 150 cities, highlighting the rapid expansion of on-demand delivery in one of the world’s fastest-growing digital markets. The expansion comes ahead of Flipkart’s Big Billion Days sale in October, as the company faces intensifying competition from [...] Since August 2024, Flipkart Minutes has built a partner ecosystem of close to 500 direct-to-consumer (D2C) brands, giving them access to hyperlocal demand and new customer segments. Through partnerships with Farmer Producer Organisations (FPOs), the platform has also connected thousands of farmers directly to consumers, helping them reach new markets and improve price realisation. [...] The new developments at Flipkart Minutes highlight the intensifying battle for scale in the country’s rapidly expanding instant-delivery market. According to a recent CLSA report, Blinkit accounted for close to 30 per cent of dark stores across the country’s top 10 cities and more than 34 per cent nationally and had the leading store count in six of them, with Zepto leading in three of the remaining four. Blinkit’s presence in more than 180 cities, CLSA said, positioned it to capture an","score":0.8430832},{"title":"'Quick' Moves To Make Flipkart Minutes Scalable","url":"https://india.entrepreneur.com/growth-strategies/quick-moves-to-make-flipkart-minutes-scalable/501272","content":"With a presence in 30-plus cities, covering close to 3,000 pincodes, Flipkart Minutes is now seen as a go-to-market channel by the Flipkart Group leadership.\n\nFlipkart Minutes was launched in August 2024, starting with select areas in Bengaluru before expanding to Delhi-NCR, Mumbai, and other cities, offering quick 10 minute deliveries for groceries and essentials by leveraging Flipkart’s logistics. [...] With a presence in 30-plus cities, covering close to 3,000 pincodes, Flipkart Minutes is now seen as a go-to-market channel by the Flipkart Group leadership.\n\nFlipkart Minutes was launched in August 2024, starting with select areas in Bengaluru before expanding to Delhi-NCR, Mumbai, and other cities, offering quick 10 minute deliveries for groceries and essentials by leveraging Flipkart’s logistics. [...] Fast forward, the quick commerce arm plays a crucial role in the overall group business, “We are optimistic about the progress at Flipkart Minutes and the business model is scalable. We are making sustainable choices and are prepared for the next phase of growth,” said Hemant Badri, senior vice president and head of supply chain, customer experience & re-commerce, Flipkart Group.","score":0.7361925},{"title":"Two years after launch, Walmart's Flipkart is closing in on India's quick-commerce leaders | TechCrunch","url":"https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/22/two-years-after-launch-walmarts-flipkart-is-closing-in-on-indias-quick-commerce-leaders","content":"Flipkart Minutes, which debuted in August 2024 as the e-commerce giant’s foray into quick commerce, is now delivering 1.1 million to 1.2 million orders a day, up from about 390,000 to 400,000 in November, people familiar with the matter told TechCrunch. That puts the two-year-old service close to Swiggy’s Instamart, which is delivering about 1.4 million orders a day, according to a person familiar with its operations. [...] Nonetheless, Flipkart has fueled that growth with an aggressive expansion of its delivery infrastructure. Minutes now operates about 1,020 to 1,050 micro-fulfillment centers — essentially small warehouses located close to customers specially to handle quick deliveries — up from 600 in January and about 340 a year ago, one of the sources told TechCrunch. The company is adding around 100 such facilities a month, the source said, aiming to have 1,500 by the end of 2026. [...] Flipkart’s advantage goes beyond adding dark stores. The company can tap an enormous pool of existing e-commerce customers it has already spent years and billions of dollars acquiring, giving Minutes a ready audience for faster deliveries, Satish Meena, an adviser at Datum Intelligence, told TechCrunch.\n\n“Flipkart is already a serious player,” Meena said. “Once you open 1,000 dark stores and [are] doing a million orders per day, it’s serious enough.”","score":0.6944897},{"title":"Flipkart","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flipkart","content":"In October 2024, Flipkart was among the initial partners in the launch of YouTube's Shopping affiliate programme in India, allowing eligible video creators to link products from Flipkart and Myntra, in their content.\n\nIn 2025, the company announced that it would shift its domicile from Singapore to India.\n\nOn 11 September 2026, Flipkart Minutes expanded its operational footprint to approximately 1,200 micro-fulfillment centers spanning over 150 cities.\n\n## Business structure\n\n[edit] [...] In March 2024, Flipkart launched its UPI services, Flipkart UPI, in partnership with Axis Bank.\n\n\n\nIn August 2024, Flipkart entered the quick-commerce market with the introduction of Flipkart Minutes. In the same month, Flipkart-backed super.money launched its mobile application offering credit-first UPI services. [...] 104. ↑ Goel, Samiksha (3 March 2024). \"Flipkart launches digital payments service Flipkart UPI\". mint. Retrieved 25 April 2024.\n105. ↑ \"Flipkart's 10-min quick-commerce service 'Minutes' goes live in Bengaluru\". Business Standard. 5 August 2024. Retrieved 28 April 2025.\n106. ↑ \"super.money launches new credit-first UPI app, hits 10 million transactions in beta phase\". CNBCTV18. 22 August 2024. Retrieved 4 May 2025.","score":0.6194488},{"title":"Flipkart Minutes | Everything in Minutes","url":"https://www.flipkart.com/flipkart-minutes-store?marketplace=HYPERLOCAL","content":"Flipkart Minutes: Get your groceries, household essentials, mobile & electronics, personal care, fruit & vegetables delivered quickly with our new rapid","score":0.59344494}]}
{"ts":"2026-09-26T17:17:22.010Z","session":"fb606d91-84d8-4449-ae8e-447eaa3b9089","event":"tool.end","callId":"e72d7b68-e620-4d58-9b5e-5587de80bec9","name":"webSearch","duration_ms":2780,"result":[{"title":"Walmart-owned Flipkart, Amazon are squeezing India’s quick-commerce startups","url":"https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/walmart-owned-flipkart-amazon-squeezing-030000591.html","content":"Flipkart, one of India's largest e-commerce players, entered quick commerce later than local rivals such as Blinkit, Swiggy, and Zepto. But it has now crossed more than 800 dark stores (distribution centers for online shopping) this week, TechCrunch has learned, and is looking to double that by the end of 2026, according to UBS. [...] Shares of Eternal, which owns Blinkit, are down about 15% so far this year, while Swiggy has fallen over 29%, even as Zepto is preparing to go public on Indian stock exchanges later this year.\n\nThe entry and expansion of large players such as Flipkart and Amazon are reshaping the competitive landscape. \"Quick commerce is no longer in a startup phase — it has become a big players' game,\" said Ankur Bisen, a senior partner at retail consultancy Technopak Advisors. [...] Nevertheless, scaling beyond big cities will take time. Quick commerce is currently viable in about 125 cities, with dark stores typically taking six to 12 months to reach maturity and profitability, said Aditya Soman, a senior research analyst at CLSA, a Hong Kong-based brokerage. Many of the newer stores in smaller towns are still in the ramp-up phase, he added.","score":0.7982658},{"title":"Quick Commerce - 2026 Market & Investments Trends","url":"https://tracxn.com/d/trending-business-models/startups-in-quick-commerce/__8Em61CsQQeo5EmPsP-wGXgDzh5yotwlRuXpxOVdqw6Y","content":"| Year | No. of companies |\n --- |\n| 2026 | 6 |\n| 2025 | 29 |\n| 2024 | 32 |\n| 2023 | 16 |\n| 2022 | 17 |\n| 2021 | 41 |\n| 2020 | 38 |\n| 2019 | 13 |\n| 2018 | 9 |\n| 2017 | 6 |\n\n### Companies founded across countries in Quick Commerce\n\nIndia has 126 Quick Commerce startups, the most by any country, followed by United States which has 16 startups and United Kingdom which has 13. [...] Logo for Talabat.com\nLogo for Swiggy\nLogo for Instacart\nLogo for Merqueo_1583836681951.png?devicePixelRatio=2&height=20&width=20)\nLogo for DoorDash\n\n## Colleges which produce the most Quick Commerce companies\n\n### Companies founded by alumni from top colleges in Quick Commerce\n\nMost number of Quick Commerce startups have been founded by alumni from IIT Delhi, IIT Bombay and IIT Kharagpur. [...] Logo for Swiggy\nLogo for Instacart\nLogo for DoorDash\nLogo for Blinkit\nLogo for Deliveroo\n\n### Companies founded year-on-year in Quick Commerce\n\n6 new startups have been founded so far in 2026 in Quick Commerce. 41 Quick Commerce startups were founded in 2021 - this is the most number of startups founded in any year in the last 10 years.","score":0.76617163},{"title":"World's Fastest Deliveries Ignite an Investment Frenzy in ...","url":"https://www.bloomberg.com/features/2025-India-instant-shopping","content":"“You couldn’t have asked for a better environment to start quick commerce in India right now,” said Pranav Pai, founding partner at Bengaluru-based venture firm 3one4 Capital. “But startups cannot keep giving discounts forever, and it’s near impossible to break even doing that.” [...] India is not alone in navigating the promises and pitfalls of quick commerce. Colombia-based Rappi Inc. stands out as a rare success story outside India, with its Turbo service offering 10-minute grocery delivery in at least seven Latin American countries, including Mexico, Brazil, and Chile. Analysts credit similar dynamics: low labor costs, urban density, and a growing digital economy. Rappi, valued at $5 billion, is now planning an IPO. [...] The ascent of Blinkit-owner Eternal into India’s benchmark BSE Sensex index in December, and then the NSE Nifty 50 in March, marked a milestone for India’s quick commerce boom. With a young consumer-tech startup in indices traditionally dominated by banks and energy giants, the inclusion reflected the sector’s growing clout.","score":0.6755297},{"title":"\"Quick commerce in India: Food and fashion verticals\" | Elevation Capital posted on the topic | LinkedIn","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/elevationcap_how-vertical-quickcommerce-is-disrupting-activity-7358452138617851906-1ggw","content":"🌯🛍️ New SummitUp episode! Quick commerce in India has evolved far beyond its grocery roots. While horizontal platforms race to add ever-growing SKUs, a new wave of vertical specialists is reimagining entire categories - delivering hot coffee and fresh snacks in 10 minutes and enabling try-before-you-buy fashion experiences. But what does it really take to build a successful vertical quick commerce startup in these categories? In this episode, Manish Advani and Ashray Iyengar from our Consumer [...] watches, learns - & enters only when the kill-shot is guaranteed. Let’s look at the board 👇 🚀 Startups: built dark stores, educated customers, proved the model. 🏬 Reliance: built the entire distribution grid - kiranas, stores, logistics, and pin codes. Now, they’re just flipping the switch: ✅ 4000+ pin codes already serviced ✅ 20,000+ kirana + retail nodes ✅ 600+ dark stores live ✅ JioMart + Reliance Retail + kirana stack = one fulfilment engine And here’s the twist They’re not chasing [...] generate a 30% higher average revenue per user (ARPU) on repeat orders. As rivals like Zappfresh push for profitability via an IPO, Licious is building a defensible hybrid moat, proving that for D2C, the future is both digital and physical. Mentions: Abhay Hanjura, Vivek Gupta, Karishma Gupta, Sajith T, Sanu Wilfred, Sayak Mitra, Yash Bhatia, Varun Khanna, Konduru Bhaskar. For daily, sharp analysis of the biggest moves in the Indian business and startup ecosystem, follow StartupFox. #Licious","score":0.6721816},{"title":"Quick commerce","url":"https://techcrunch.com/tag/quick-commerce","content":"Disrupt 2026: OpenAI, Anthropic, Replit, and more take over 6 industry stages. 25% off tickets now\n\nDisrupt ticket savings of up to $200 + 50% off a second ends Sept 25, 11:59 p.m. PT. REGISTER HERE.\n\nLatest\n\nAI\n\nAmazon\n\nApps\n\nBiotech & Health\n\nClimate\n\nCloud Computing\n\nCommerce\n\nCrypto\n\nEnterprise\n\nEVs\n\nFintech\n\nFundraising\n\nGadgets\n\nGaming\n\nGoogle\n\nGovernment & Policy\n\nHardware\n\nInstagram\n\nLayoffs\n\nMedia & Entertainment\n\nMeta\n\nMicrosoft\n\nPrivacy\n\nRobotics\n\nSecurity\n\nSocial\n\nSpace\n\nStartups [...] OpenAI caught its models leaving notes to successors to hide bad behavior\n\nMicrosoft exec called AI scraping ‘the largest theft of labor in human history,’ new unredacted filings reveal\n\nAutomattic’s interim CEO and legal chief signed reciprocal severance deals during Mullenweg’s brief ouster\n\nFormer Infosys chief’s AI startup nabs another $53M\n\nClean tech startup Fluxnium found a way to tap 50,000 years’ worth of nuclear fuel [...] Space\n\nStartups\n\nTikTok\n\nTransportation\n\nVenture\n\nStaff\n\nEvents\n\nStartup Battlefield\n\nStrictlyVC\n\nNewsletters\n\nPodcasts\n\nVideos\n\nPartner Content\n\nTechCrunch Brand Studio\n\nContact Us\n\nLast day to book an exhibit table is September 18. Don’t miss out on high-impact leads, investor access, and a brand spotlight in Disrupt’s Expo Hall.\n\nTilly Norwood’s press tour is going about as well as you’d expect for an AI\n\nA new kind of AI model from a ChatGPT inventor is thrilling developers","score":0.21398467}]}
